# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
miracle	one
violates	one
observed	one
uniform	one
proof	one
# The likeliest candidate for a shift is "miracle". S1 defines it as a violation of the laws of nature; S3 to S5 pick it out by its never having been observed.
# The text treats the never-observed as the mark of such a violation, so I read this as one concept described two ways, not two senses.
# Likewise, "uniform experience" in A1 (never observed) and in P2 (the experience that established the laws) seems to be the same claim.
# "Proof" is Hume's technical term throughout: an argument from experience that leaves no room for doubt.
